   "heading": "Epidemic curves",
   "segment_index": "S1.P1",
   "kind": "untrue",
   "en_span": "chapters/epicurves.qmd:17-17",
   "tr_span": "chapters/epicurves.tr.qmd:17-20",
   "proposition": "The translation states that the page demonstrates two approaches to making epicurves in R, the first being the incidence2 package, which can build an epicurve with simple commands; the English states only that the page demonstrates epicurves with ggplot2.",
   "reader_consequence": "A Turkish reader expects an incidence2 walk-through that appears nowhere in the chapter, and may abandon ggplot2 while searching for the easier commands promised in the introduction.",
   "confidence": "high"
  },
  {
   "heading": "Epidemic curves",
   "segment_index": "S1.P1",
   "kind": "missing",
   "en_span": "chapters/epicurves.qmd:27-27",
   "tr_span": "chapters/epicurves.tr.qmd:28-29",
   "proposition": "The English states that incidence2 was undergoing revisions, was not stable at the time of writing, and will be re-added to the chapter when stable; the translation omits this note entirely.",
   "reader_consequence": "A Turkish reader is given no reason why incidence2 content is absent, and may install and rely on an unstable package instead of waiting for the revised chapter.",
   "confidence": "high"
  },
  {
   "heading": "Epicurves with ggplot2",
   "segment_index": "S7.P1",
   "kind": "added",
   "en_span": "chapters/epicurves.qmd:146-146",
   "tr_span": "chapters/epicurves.tr.qmd:146-146",
   "proposition": "The translation opens the sentence with 'Unlike using the incidence2 package', framing the manual control of time aggregation and date-axis label intervals as a contrast with incidence2; the English makes the statement without any comparison.",
   "reader_consequence": "A Turkish reader infers that incidence2 handles the aggregation and date labels automatically and that this chapter has shown it, when the chapter never covers incidence2 at all.",
   "confidence": "high"
  },
  {
   "heading": "Weekly epicurve example",
   "segment_index": "S9.P1",
   "kind": "missing",
   "en_span": "chapters/epicurves.qmd:280-280",
   "tr_span": "chapters/epicurves.tr.qmd:282-283",
   "proposition": "The English bullet list states that closed = \"left\" is used in geom_histogram() so that dates are counted into the correct bins; the translation's bullet list omits this point.",
   "reader_consequence": "A Turkish reader does not learn that bin edges must be closed on the left, so cases falling exactly on a Monday break point are silently assigned to the previous week and every weekly count can be wrong by the boundary cases.",
   "confidence": "high"
  },
  {
   "heading": "Multi-level date labels",
   "segment_index": "S29.P1",
   "kind": "missing",
   "en_span": "chapters/epicurves.qmd:1341-1341",
   "tr_span": "chapters/epicurves.tr.qmd:1314-1315",
   "proposition": "The English states that the easiest method is to assign the labels = argument of scale_x_date() to scales::label_date_short(), which builds efficient date labels automatically and adjusts them as the data expand from days to weeks to months and years; the translation omits this whole method.",
   "reader_consequence": "A Turkish reader loses the simplest solution to the stated problem and is left only with the faceting workaround, which is far more code for the same result.",
   "confidence": "high"
  },
  {
   "heading": "Multi-level date labels",
   "segment_index": "S29.P2",
   "kind": "missing",
   "en_span": "chapters/epicurves.qmd:1371-1371",
